Start by defining the public API. A cache has three operations: `get`, `put`, and `size`. The generic type parameters let callers store any key-value pair. Internally, each entry lives in a `Node` that carries `prev` / `next` pointers for the linked list plus the key (needed during eviction to remove the entry from the map).

```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {

    private final int capacity;
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, Node<K, V>> map;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    // Sentinel nodes simplify edge cases — head.next is MRU, tail.prev is LRU
    private final Node<K, V> head = new Node<>(null, null);
    private final Node<K, V> tail = new Node<>(null, null);

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(capacity);
        head.next = tail;
        tail.prev = head;
    }

    private static class Node<K, V> {
        final K key;
        volatile V value;  // volatile for safe publication
        Node<K, V> prev;
        Node<K, V> next;

        Node(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }
}
```

Sentinel nodes (`head` and `tail`) eliminate null-checks when inserting or removing at boundaries. The most-recently-used entry always sits at `head.next`; the eviction candidate sits at `tail.prev`. This convention keeps every list operation uniform.

public V get(K key) {
    Node<K, V> node = map.get(key);  // ConcurrentHashMap: lock-free read
    if (node == null) return null;

    // Move to head requires list mutation → write lock
    lock.writeLock().lock();
    try {
        moveToHead(node);
    } finally {
        lock.writeLock().unlock();
    }
    return node.value;
}

public void put(K key, V value) {
    Node<K, V> existing = map.get(key);

    if (existing != null) {
        existing.value = value;  // update in place
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            moveToHead(existing);
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
        return;
    }

    // New entry
    Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
    lock.writeLock().lock();
    try {
        addAfterHead(newNode);
        map.put(key, newNode);

        if (map.size() > capacity) {
            Node<K, V> lru = tail.prev;  // least recently used
            removeNode(lru);
            map.remove(lru.key);          // evict from map too
        }
    } finally {
        lock.writeLock().unlock();
    }
}

// ---- linked-list helpers (caller must hold write lock) ----

private void addAfterHead(Node<K, V> node) {
    node.prev = head;
    node.next = head.next;
    head.next.prev = node;
    head.next = node;
}

private void removeNode(Node<K, V> node) {
    node.prev.next = node.next;
    node.next.prev = node.prev;
}

private void moveToHead(Node<K, V> node) {
    removeNode(node);  // unlink from current position
    addAfterHead(node); // re-insert at head
}

The `ScheduledTask` class wraps a `Runnable` with scheduling metadata. It implements `Delayed` so it can sit in a `DelayQueue`, and `Comparable` for priority ordering within the same time window.

```java
public class ScheduledTask implements Delayed, Comparable<ScheduledTask> {

    private final String taskId;
    private final Runnable command;
    private final int priority;          // lower = higher priority
    private long nextExecutionNanos;      // absolute nanoTime
    private final long periodNanos;       // 0 = one-shot, >0 = periodic
    private volatile boolean cancelled = false;

    public ScheduledTask(String taskId, Runnable command,
                         int priority, long delayMs, long periodMs) {
        this.taskId = taskId;
        this.command = command;
        this.priority = priority;
        this.nextExecutionNanos = System.nanoTime()
            + TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toNanos(delayMs);
        this.periodNanos = TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toNanos(periodMs);
    }

    @Override
    public long getDelay(TimeUnit unit) {
        return unit.convert(
            nextExecutionNanos - System.nanoTime(), TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(ScheduledTask other) {
        // Primary: execution time. Secondary: priority.
        int timeCmp = Long.compare(this.nextExecutionNanos,
                                    other.nextExecutionNanos);
        return timeCmp != 0 ? timeCmp
            : Integer.compare(this.priority, other.priority);
    }

    public boolean isPeriodic() { return periodNanos > 0; }

    public void advanceExecution() {
        nextExecutionNanos += periodNanos; // fixed-rate scheduling
    }

    public void cancel() { this.cancelled = true; }
    public boolean isCancelled() { return cancelled; }
    public Runnable getCommand() { return command; }
    public String getTaskId() { return taskId; }
}
```

Using `System.nanoTime()` instead of `System.currentTimeMillis()` avoids clock skew from NTP adjustments. Periodic tasks call `advanceExecution()` to set the next fire time, which implements **fixed-rate** scheduling. For **fixed-delay**, you would set `nextExecutionNanos = System.nanoTime() + periodNanos` after each run completes.

public class TaskScheduler {

    private final DelayQueue<ScheduledTask> queue = new DelayQueue<>();
    private final ExecutorService workerPool;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public TaskScheduler(int poolSize) {
        this.workerPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(poolSize);
        Thread dispatcher = new Thread(this::dispatchLoop,
                                        "scheduler-dispatcher");
        dispatcher.setDaemon(true);  // don't block JVM shutdown
        dispatcher.start();
    }

    public void schedule(ScheduledTask task) {
        queue.put(task);  // thread-safe, non-blocking
    }

    private void dispatchLoop() {
        while (running) {
            try {
                // Blocks until a task's delay expires
                ScheduledTask task = queue.take();

                if (task.isCancelled()) continue;

                workerPool.submit(() -> {
                    try {
                        task.getCommand().run();
                    } catch (Exception e) {
                        // Log but don't kill the scheduler
                        System.err.println("Task "
                            + task.getTaskId() + " failed: " + e);
                    } finally {
                        if (task.isPeriodic() && !task.isCancelled()) {
                            task.advanceExecution();
                            queue.put(task);  // re-enqueue
                        }
                    }
                });
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        running = false;
        workerPool.shutdown();
    }
}

public class ConnectionPool {

    private final ConcurrentLinkedDeque<PooledConnection> idle
        = new ConcurrentLinkedDeque<>();
    private final Set<PooledConnection> active
        = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();  // track active for leak detection
    private final Semaphore permits;       // bounds total connections
    private final String jdbcUrl;
    private final int maxSize;
    private final long borrowTimeoutMs;

    public ConnectionPool(String jdbcUrl, int maxSize,
                          long borrowTimeoutMs) {
        this.jdbcUrl = jdbcUrl;
        this.maxSize = maxSize;
        this.borrowTimeoutMs = borrowTimeoutMs;
        this.permits = new Semaphore(maxSize, true); // fair ordering
    }

    public Connection borrow() throws SQLException {
        try {
            // Block if all connections are in use
            if (!permits.tryAcquire(borrowTimeoutMs,
                                     TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                throw new SQLException(
                    "Connection pool exhausted, waited "
                    + borrowTimeoutMs + "ms");
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            throw new SQLException("Interrupted waiting for connection");
        }

        PooledConnection conn = idle.pollFirst();

        // No idle connection available → create new one
        if (conn == null || !conn.isHealthy()) {
            conn = createNewConnection();
        }

        conn.markBorrowed();  // record checkout timestamp
        active.add(conn);
        return conn.getConnection();
    }

    public void release(Connection connection) {
        PooledConnection pooled = findPooled(connection);
        active.remove(pooled);
        pooled.markReturned();

        if (pooled.isHealthy()) {
            idle.addFirst(pooled);  // LIFO: reuse hot connections
        } else {
            pooled.closeQuietly();  // discard and allow new creation
        }
        permits.release();  // allow another thread to borrow
    }

    private PooledConnection createNewConnection()
            throws SQLException {
        Connection raw = DriverManager.getConnection(jdbcUrl);
        return new PooledConnection(raw);
    }
}

public class EventBus {

    // Event type → list of subscribers for that type
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Class<?>,
        CopyOnWriteArrayList<Subscription<?>>> registry
            = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    private final ExecutorService asyncPool
        = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(
            Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(),
            r -> {
                Thread t = new Thread(r, "eventbus-worker");
                t.setDaemon(true);  // don't block JVM shutdown
                return t;
            });

    // Type-safe subscribe: returns token for unsubscription
    public <E> Subscription<E> subscribe(Class<E> eventType,
                                          Consumer<E> handler) {
        Subscription<E> sub = new Subscription<>(eventType, handler);
        registry.computeIfAbsent(eventType,
            k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>()).add(sub);
        return sub;  // caller keeps this to unsubscribe later
    }

    public void unsubscribe(Subscription<?> sub) {
        var subs = registry.get(sub.getEventType());
        if (subs != null) subs.remove(sub);
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <E> void publish(E event) {
        var subs = registry.get(event.getClass());
        if (subs == null) return;

        for (Subscription<?> sub : subs) {
            Subscription<E> typed = (Subscription<E>) sub;
            asyncPool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    typed.getHandler().accept(event);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    // Isolate: one failing subscriber
                    // must not affect others
                    System.err.println("Subscriber failed: " + e);
                }
            });
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        asyncPool.shutdown();
    }
}

// Subscription token — immutable, used for unsubscribe
public class Subscription<E> {
    private final Class<E> eventType;
    private final Consumer<E> handler;

    Subscription(Class<E> eventType, Consumer<E> handler) {
        this.eventType = eventType;
        this.handler = handler;
    }

    Class<E> getEventType() { return eventType; }
    Consumer<E> getHandler() { return handler; }
}

**Why CopyOnWriteArrayList?** Subscriptions change rarely (at startup/shutdown) but are iterated on every publish. COWAL is optimized for exactly this read-heavy pattern — iteration never locks, only mutations copy the internal array. If subscriptions change frequently at runtime, switch to a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` instead.

**Event hierarchy:** the implementation above uses exact-type matching — publishing an `OrderCreatedEvent` does not notify subscribers of its superclass `DomainEvent`. To support hierarchy dispatch, walk the class hierarchy at publish time:

```java
public <E> void publishWithHierarchy(E event) {
    Class<?> type = event.getClass();
    while (type != null) {
        var subs = registry.get(type);
        if (subs != null) {
            for (Subscription<?> sub : subs) {
                asyncPool.submit(() -> {
                    try {
                        ((Consumer) sub.getHandler()).accept(event);
                    } catch (Exception e) {
                        System.err.println("Subscriber failed: " + e);
                    }
                });
            }
        }
        type = type.getSuperclass(); // walk up the hierarchy
    }
}
```

Guava EventBus uses this approach with a cache of type hierarchies to avoid repeated reflection. Spring ApplicationEventPublisher resolves the full type hierarchy plus implemented interfaces, which is why a listener on `ApplicationEvent` receives every event.

public class TTLKeyValueStore<K, V> {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, Entry<V>> store
        = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    private final ScheduledExecutorService cleaner
        = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor(r -> {
            Thread t = new Thread(r, "ttl-cleaner");
            t.setDaemon(true);
            return t;
        });

    public TTLKeyValueStore() {
        // Active expiration: sweep every second
        cleaner.scheduleAtFixedRate(
            this::expireRandomSample,
            1, 1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    public void put(K key, V value, long ttlMs) {
        long expiresAt = System.currentTimeMillis() + ttlMs;
        store.put(key, new Entry<>(value, expiresAt));
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        Entry<V> entry = store.get(key);
        if (entry == null) return null;

        // Lazy expiration: check on every read
        if (entry.isExpired()) {
            store.remove(key, entry);  // atomic: only remove this entry
            return null;
        }
        return entry.value;
    }

    public boolean delete(K key) {
        return store.remove(key) != null;
    }

    public int size() {
        return store.size(); // includes expired-but-not-yet-evicted
    }

    // Redis-style probabilistic sweep
    private void expireRandomSample() {
        var keys = store.keySet().toArray();
        if (keys.length == 0) return;

        int sampleSize = Math.min(20, keys.length);
        int expired = 0;
        ThreadLocalRandom rng = ThreadLocalRandom.current();

        for (int i = 0; i < sampleSize; i++) {
            @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
            K key = (K) keys[rng.nextInt(keys.length)];
            Entry<V> entry = store.get(key);
            if (entry != null && entry.isExpired()) {
                store.remove(key, entry);
                expired++;
            }
        }

        // If >25% expired, run again immediately
        if (expired > sampleSize / 4) {
            expireRandomSample(); // recursive burst
        }
    }

    private record Entry<V>(V value, long expiresAt) {
        boolean isExpired() {
            return System.currentTimeMillis() >= expiresAt;
        }
    }
}

public class TokenBucketRateLimiter {

    private final int maxTokens;           // bucket capacity
    private final double refillPerNano;    // tokens per nanosecond
    private double availableTokens;
    private long lastRefillNanos;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public TokenBucketRateLimiter(int maxTokens,
                                  int refillPerSecond) {
        this.maxTokens = maxTokens;
        this.refillPerNano = refillPerSecond / 1_000_000_000.0;
        this.availableTokens = maxTokens;  // start full
        this.lastRefillNanos = System.nanoTime();
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire() {
        return tryAcquire(1);
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire(int tokens) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            refill();
            if (availableTokens >= tokens) {
                availableTokens -= tokens;
                return true;
            }
            return false; // rejected
        }
    }

    private void refill() {
        long now = System.nanoTime();
        long elapsed = now - lastRefillNanos;
        double newTokens = elapsed * refillPerNano;
        // Cap at maxTokens — never exceed bucket size
        availableTokens = Math.min(maxTokens,
                                    availableTokens + newTokens);
        lastRefillNanos = now;
    }
}

A fixed-window counter resets at every boundary. A client can send 100 requests at 0:59 and 100 more at 1:00, achieving 200 in one second while the per-minute limit is 100. Sliding window fixes this by using overlapping sub-windows.

```java
public class SlidingWindowRateLimiter {

    private final int maxRequests;        // allowed per window
    private final long windowMs;          // e.g., 60_000 for 1 minute
    private final long subWindowMs;       // e.g., 1_000 for 1-second slices
    private final int[] counters;         // circular buffer of sub-windows
    private long currentSubWindow;        // index of current sub-window
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public SlidingWindowRateLimiter(int maxRequests,
                                    long windowMs,
                                    long subWindowMs) {
        this.maxRequests = maxRequests;
        this.windowMs = windowMs;
        this.subWindowMs = subWindowMs;
        int numSubWindows = (int) (windowMs / subWindowMs);
        this.counters = new int[numSubWindows];
        this.currentSubWindow = System.currentTimeMillis() / subWindowMs;
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            advanceWindow();
            int total = 0;
            for (int c : counters) total += c; // sum all sub-windows

            if (total >= maxRequests) return false;

            counters[currentIndex()]++;
            return true;
        }
    }

    private void advanceWindow() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis() / subWindowMs;
        long diff = now - currentSubWindow;
        if (diff > 0) {
            // Zero out stale sub-windows
            for (long i = 0; i < Math.min(diff, counters.length); i++) {
                counters[(int) ((currentSubWindow + i + 1)
                    % counters.length)] = 0;
            }
            currentSubWindow = now;
        }
    }

    private int currentIndex() {
        return (int) (currentSubWindow % counters.length);
    }
}
```

The circular buffer uses modulo indexing so memory is constant regardless of uptime. When the window advances, stale sub-windows are zeroed out. The sum of all sub-windows gives the request count over the last `windowMs` milliseconds — a true sliding window.

In production, each client (identified by API key, IP, or user ID) needs its own rate limiter instance. A `ConcurrentHashMap<String, RateLimiter>` does the job:

```java
